# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Interactivity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

let jsDemoOut = paragraph("Nothing has happened yet.") |> elementId("js-demo-out")

let jsDemoIdle = "Nothing has happened yet."
let jsDemoDone = "That was real JavaScript, in a static page."

let jsDemoButton = primaryButton("Run some JavaScript")
	|> onClick([
		jsConst("o", jsGetElementById("js-demo-out")),
		jsSetText(jsIdent("o"), jsTernary(
			jsEq(jsMember(jsIdent("o"), "textContent"), jsStr(jsDemoIdle)),
			jsStr(jsDemoDone),
			jsStr(jsDemoIdle)))
	])

let interactive = docSection("interactive", "Interactivity", [
	paragraph("A static page has no Caja runtime in the browser — main.caja ran at build time and produced HTML. So there are two different kinds of event handler, and only one of them works here."),
	subheading("The one that works in a static page"),
	paragraph("onClick takes JavaScript and renders it as an inline event attribute. The browser runs it; nothing of Caja's is involved at runtime. Press the button:"),
	inlineRow([jsDemoButton]),
	jsDemoOut,
	subheading("The JavaScript is built, not written"),
	paragraph("@caja/js builds JavaScript the way @caja/ui builds HTML: a tree of expressions and statements, rendered to source at the end. The button above is this — note that the two messages are ordinary Caja values, shared between the page text and the script."),
	codeSample("import * from \"@caja/js\"\n\nlet idle = \"Nothing has happened yet.\"\nlet done = \"That was real JavaScript.\"\n\nlet b = primaryButton(\"Run it\")\n\t|> onClick([\n\t\tjsConst(\"o\", jsGetElementById(\"out\")),\n\t\tjsSetText(jsIdent(\"o\"), jsTernary(\n\t\t\tjsEq(jsMember(jsIdent(\"o\"), \"textContent\"), jsStr(idle)),\n\t\t\tjsStr(done),\n\t\t\tjsStr(idle)))\n\t])"),
	paragraph("That is the part a plain string cannot do: the script depends on values your Caja program computed. jsStr escapes whatever you give it, so data crossing into JavaScript — a page list, a label, anything with a quote in it — is inert text rather than syntax."),
	subheading("Or write it by hand, checked"),
	paragraph("For JavaScript you would rather just write, js.raw takes a literal and parses it at compile time with a real JavaScript parser. A missing bracket is a Caja build error pointing at the call, not a page that loads and quietly does nothing:"),
	codeSample("import js\n\nonClick([jsRawStmt(js.raw(\"alert('saved')\"))])"),
	mutedText("js.raw's argument must be a string literal, because that is what puts the source in front of the validator while the compiler is still running. A script assembled at runtime could not be checked, so it is rejected rather than silently trusted. jsRawStmt is the only node that carries unescaped syntax, and it can only hold something js.raw produced."),
	paragraph("There is one onX function per event, matching the onX set: onInput, onSubmit, onKeyDown, and so on. For JavaScript that is not one element's handler — a shared helper, some setup on load — scriptBlock takes the same statement list and emits a script element instead."),
	subheading("The one that does not"),
	paragraph("onClick and its siblings take a Caja function. Those need a runtime to attach them, which only a web-app build (compiled to WebAssembly, mounted with @caja/dom) has. Render one to a static page and it is dropped, with a warning at build time telling you so. The same component code is safe in both kinds of project; the handler simply does nothing in this one."),
	mutedText("Neither kind of handler can read a variable from your Caja program. A script is text sent to a browser, and a Caja handler cannot close over an outer let either — Caja's purity rules forbid it. Read what you need from the page inside the handler.")
])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Building your own theme
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

let ownTheme = docSection("theme", "Building your own theme", [
	paragraph("siriguela is one opinion about how a page should look, and nothing forces you to keep it. A theme of your own is an ordinary .caja module that imports @caja/ui — the structural layer — and exports its own components, exactly as this one does."),
	paragraph("Build on @caja/ui rather than on siriguela, so you are not inheriting a palette you are about to replace."),
	codeSample("import \"@caja/ui\" as ui\n\nconst brandPrimary = \"#1D7A85\"\n\nconst brandButton = fn(label: String) -> ui.View {\n\treturn ui.button(label)\n\t\t|> ui.backgroundColor(brandPrimary)\n\t\t|> ui.color(\"#FFFFFF\")\n\t\t|> ui.rounded(\"6px\")\n\t\t|> ui.padding(\"8px 16px\")\n}"),
	mutedText("brandPrimary is a const, not a let: Caja's purity rules let a function read an outer const but not an outer let — a real compile error brandButton would hit immediately otherwise. Note also that everything is reached qualified, types included (ui.View). Nothing is imported by name, which is what keeps a theme from re-exporting the layer it is built on: a named import is forwarded to your consumers, a qualified one is not."),
	paragraph("@caja/ui's README documents the full structural catalog a theme is built from: the View tree, the modifier chain, the component catalog, and the CSS-in-Caja APIs for writing a stylesheet like the one siteStylesheet returns.")
])

